  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Ethiopia Drought Acute JR 2022</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Ethiopia is experiencing its worst drought in 40 years following 4 consecutive failed rainy seasons. Somali and Oromia states are most affected, 3.5 and 3.4 million people respectively, with Somali receiving the greatest aid inflow. Several zones in Oromia have received no rainfall in the last 2 years leading to large-scale livestock deaths, limited livestock births and decrease in livestock price as they are in poor condition. Women and children, particularly girls, are suffering most due to child labor, lack of access to food, water and health services, and other protection concerns. The risk of Gender-Based Violence (GBV) for women and girls has increased as they are struggling to secure HH water, food, and fuel. Children and youth are increasingly engaged in child labor to meet HH food needs (ibid). 
 Save the Children (SC) and local partner AFD will respond in Laga Hidha and Rayitu woredas of East Bale Zone. East Bale has the highest percentage (51.2%) of drought affected population in Oromia (Oromia State Drought RP 22). Laga Hidha and Rayitu are among the most affected woredas of the zone (NDRMC, March 22). SC will complement ongoing food assistance with Multi-Purpose Cash (MPC) and will support livelihood restoration through the provision of drought resistant seeds</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="3">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">HHs severely affected by drought and categorized as IPC 3+ will be targeted based on sector-agreed poverty and vulnerability criteria based on HEA baseline, prioritizing female and child -headed HHs, PLWG, adolescent girls, PWDs and the elderly not living with their children. Special attention will be given to ensure PWDs, women and children are included and receive SBCC messages on nutrition and health. SC will ensure safe time and location for cash and seed distribution to ensure accessibility by vulnerable groups. SC/AFD targets both IDPs (25%) and host populations (75%) and conducts contextual analysis to ensure inclusive and conflict-sensitive programming.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="2">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">&quot;The intervention is based on cluster recommendations, expertise of SC/AFD and complementarity and gaps with ongoing interventions. IRC delivers emergency WASH while Safety Net Program runs with incomplete food baskets. SC/AFD implement emergency WASH, Nutrition, Education, Protection and development activities. SC/AFD will provide 3 monthly MPC transfers of ETB 4500 to 959 farmer and agro-pastoralist HHs (5754 ind.) Based on community consultations, SC will use bank transfer or mobile money.  The provision of MPC will reduce food insecurity and protection risks for women and girls. 
The project will also support 430 MPC beneficiary HHs (2580 ind.) with certified drought tolerant, high yielding and nutritious maize, beans and vegetable seeds, to be distributed at the beginning of the planting season of Hagaya rains (October-December).</narrative>
  </description>
